{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1166725-what-film-would-you-use-to-represent-splat-x","id":1166725,"title":"What film would you use to represent splat X","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-11T09:13:10-08:00","id":1166725,"title":"What film would you use to represent splat X","content":"This is a bit of an inversion of the other thread on how to explain different media in WoD terms. Instead, I'd like to see what shows, movies, books etc to represent a splat or sub splat. I've found the that when I suggest a campaign, WoD or otherwise, one of the first questions I get is &quot;What should I watch to get in the right mood/get ideas from&quot;.<br /> <br /> Since I'm the one asking, I'll do a couple of ones I've used in the past <br /> <br /> Fomori - X-files season 7 episode 3 &quot;Hungry&quot;, a fast food worker develops an appatite for human brains and finds himself turning into a freakish monster.<br /> <br /> Heroic Technocrat MiB vs Nephandi - also X-files, I think it's season 8 episode 14 &quot;This Is Not Happening&quot;. A child with control over reality torments his parents. Agent Doggett fights him by denying/disbelieving the kid's power, while also using the child's power against him.<br /> <br /> My upcoming campaign is going to be Changeling, set in an Elementary/Middle school that also happens to be a renovated hospital with a dark past. Obviously that's super specific, but I'd like to see what others think could represent a similar premise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23589-jimalcoatl","name":"Jimalcoatl","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"23"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T10:02:38-08:00","id":1166740,"title":"","content":"For Mage we have this thread:<br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/600447-the-collected-fiction-inspirations-for-mage-the-ascension\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/filedata/fetch?filedataid=1074\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/600447-the-collected-fiction-inspirations-for-mage-the-ascension\"> The collected Fiction Inspirations for Mage: The Ascension - Onyx Path Forums </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/600447-the-collected-fiction-inspirations-for-mage-the-ascension\"> http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/600447-the-collected-fiction-inspirations-for-mage-the-ascension </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> I'd like to request the mods to sticky this, so that we don't have one to two topics each month on what makes a good Mage resource. This is just the listing, the </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T12:15:59-08:00","id":1166766,"title":"","content":"For Vampire:<ul><li>Bram Stoker's Dracula (the book, not the film)</li> <li>Anne Rice's Interview with the Vampire, The Vampire Lestat and Queen of the Damned</li> <li>Oscar Wilde's The Picture of Dorian Grey</li> <li>F. Scott Fitzgerald's The Beautiful and the Damned</li> <li>The Godfather (1972) and The Godfather Part II (1974)</li> <li>Scarface (1983)</li> <li>Breaking Bad (2008-2013)</li> <li>Trainspotting (1996)</li> <li>Gridlock'd (1997)</li> <li>Shadow of the Vampire (2000)</li> </ul>The first two because they're where most of the game's original material derives from; the rest all have to do with moral decay, the corruption of power, addiction, and obsession. <br /> <br /> For Werewolf:<ul><li>The Wolfen (1981)</li> <li>Prophecy (1979)</li> <li>CHUD (1984)</li> <li>An American Werewolf in London (1981)</li> <li>Wolf (1994)</li> <li>Dog Soldiers (2002)</li> <li>Wer (2014)</li> <li>Never Cry Wolf (1983)</li> <li>Evil Dead (2013)</li> <li>Splinter (2008)</li> <li>Primal (2010)</li> </ul>The first four are major influences on the original game in one way or another, especially in the realm of toxic chemicals creating mutated monsters. The next two, IMO, do a decent job of showcasing certain aspects of the Garou as far as behavior, pack tactics, and defending territory. Wer, I feel, is an almost perfect look at a lot of the game's themes from a human outsider's point of view. Never Cry Wolf was a landmark in shifting public perspectives about wolves. The last three, to me, at least, represent a world that is often spiritually and physically hostile to humans. <br /> <br /> In addition, there's a fairly decent amount of respectable werewolf fiction out there, usually short story form, including works by Robert E. Howard, Algernon Blackwood, Saki, Manly Wade Wellman, Rudyard Kipling, and Sir Arthur Conan Doyle. Also, Alan Moore's run on Swamp Thing. <br /> <br /> For Mage:<ul><li>Mary Shelley's Frankenstein</li> <li>Grant Morrison's The Invisibles</li> <li>Alan Moore's Promethea</li> <li>Jim Butcher's The Dresdon Files series</li> <li>Lev Grossman's The Magicians</li> <li>Clive Barker's The Great &amp; Secret Show (among others)</li> <li>William Gibson's Blue Ant trilogy (Pattern Recognition, Spook Country, Zero History) as well as his Bridge trilogy and Sprawl trilogy.</li> <li>Dean Koontz's The Silent Corner, his Frankenstein series, his Odd Thomas series and many others</li> <li>Lord of Illusions (1995)</li> <li>Dark City (1998)</li> <li>Pi (1998)</li> <li>The Gift (2000)</li> <li>The Craft (1996)</li> <li>The Adjustment Bureau (2011)</li> <li>Fight Club (1999)</li> <li>The Matrix (1999) - more for style than for substance</li> </ul><br /> For The Arcanum:<ul><li>Anne Rice's The Witching Hour, as the entire sect is derived from the Talamasca</li> <li>The Ninth Gate (1999)</li> <li>Poltergeist (1984)</li> </ul>For the World of Darkness in general:<ul><li>The Terminator (1984)</li> <li>The Devil's Advocate (1997)</li> <li>Robocop (1987)</li> <li>Ghost Dog: The Way of the Samurai (1999)</li> <li>Seven (1995)</li> <li>Strange Days (1995)</li> <li>Candyman (1992)</li> <li>John Carpenter's apocalypse trilogy, The Thing (1982), Prince of Darkness (1987), and In the Mouth of Madness (1995)</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T12:58:28-08:00","id":1166775,"title":"","content":"I remember back in the 90s Richard Dansky referred to Peter Jackson's <i>The Frighteners</i> as <i>Wraith: The Movie</i>.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-11T18:18:36-08:00","id":1166868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1166725-what-film-would-you-use-to-represent-splat-x?p=1166775#post1166775\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I remember back in the 90s Richard Dansky referred to Peter Jackson's <i>The Frighteners</i> as <i>Wraith: The Movie</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. That and to a degree Ghost where the big ones.<br /> <br /> Also Jacob's Ladder had a lot of fans among Wraith writers as an example of harrowings.<br /> <br /> I personally enjoyed some of Kafka's work as inspiration for dealing with certain aspects of Hierarchy bureaucracy. Likewise the movie Gridlock'd.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","